00:03:36.958 --> 00:03:38.291
CACHE MCWHERTER: Thanks, Sophia.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.580
The first things
that you need to do

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.170
to get any kind of
security for your cloud

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.890
is to essentially
root your trust

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:52.070
in the identities
in your system.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.560
Without securing your identities
and the identities that

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.570
can access your
resources, there really

00:03:56.570 --> 00:04:01.540
is no hope in securing
the rest of your cloud.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.530
And that means that you
need an identity store,

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.696
you need an identity provider,
and you need policies

00:04:05.696 --> 00:04:08.830
that you can apply
to those identities.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.704
Here at Google,
we have solutions

00:04:11.704 --> 00:04:14.120
to get you started here for
no matter what kind of company

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.830
you are.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.480
And the first way that you--

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:22.180
and two general paths in which
you can establish your identity

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.350
store at Google.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:27.110
The first is to use Google as
your identity provider itself.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:30.260
And I'm sure that many
of you do this already.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.490
Google does this internally.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:34.680
We use our products
internally all the time.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.510
The basic is a fully-fledged
identity system

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.120
with groups, and users,
and APIs to manage them,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:43.730
security controls, and
so forth, and so on.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.610
And it's also conveniently
very easy to set up

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:52.370
for small customers
of Google Cloud.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:54.350
If you're an established
shop, and you

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.990
have an existing identity
system, such as Active

00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:58.790
Directory, or an
LDAP system, you

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:01.994
can use Google
Cloud Directory Sync

00:05:01.994 --> 00:05:04.160
to synchronize those
identities and groups to Google

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.471
and use them and apply them in
policies in the Google Cloud

00:05:07.471 --> 00:05:07.970
product.

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:13.200
The next thing, after you
establish your identity store

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.990
at Google Cloud, you need
to secure them somehow.

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:19.990
And fortunately at
Google, we've basically

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:23.100
been working on securing
clouds for many, many years

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.510
now, first with our
internal cloud product

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:29.040
but also for now with our
external cloud products.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.790
We've been dealing with attacks
on our internal resources

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.360
and our customers'
resources from state

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.980
actors, non-state actors.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.850
You name it, we've
probably seen it.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:45.769
And we have a lot of researchers
and security engineers just

00:05:45.769 --> 00:05:47.310
tracking all of the
instances that we

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.610
can find of things maybe
the rest of the world

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.060
hasn't even discovered yet.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.490
And we then also work on trying
to mitigate those attacks

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:01.080
with new tooling and
features that sometimes we

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.760
apply internally but
then sometimes we

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:06.646
give to you to apply
to your own resources.

00:06:06.646 --> 00:06:08.520
And I'm going to give
you a list of basically

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.340
a few of the things that I think
that you should be turning on

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.210
basically out of the box for
ensuring that your cloud is

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.340
secure.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.840
The first thing I
want to talk about

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:23.085
is two-factor authentication
and security keys.

00:06:23.085 --> 00:06:24.900
We have been using
these internally

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.550
for a couple of years now.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.370
And they have been super
effective at eliminating

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:33.510
all sorts of account attacks
and phishing attacks.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:37.060
The basic idea is that
your users get a--

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:39.030
your developers
get a security key.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.880
And this is a hard to
phish security device

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.300
that has to be present every
time that your users log in,

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.532
your developers log in.

00:06:46.532 --> 00:06:48.240
And so what that means
is that if someone

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.530
can steal my password or trick
me into answering questions,

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.540
an attacker can't
actually log in

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.200
unless they're in possession
of the security device as well.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.270
To configure your
systems to use this,

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:05.005
you can go to the Google
Cloud Admin console,

00:07:05.005 --> 00:07:06.630
and you can go to
the security settings

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:09.365
and turn on the security keys.

00:07:09.365 --> 00:07:10.240
This is so important.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.100
We've actually just announced
our own security key products,

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.724
which should be coming to market
at some point in the future.

00:07:17.724 --> 00:07:19.890
The second thing, after
you've secured your accounts

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.920
themselves, is to secure
your connected applications

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:25.560
to your identities.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.130
I'm sure all of you have seen
the news with various incidents

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.720
that basically, if a third-party
application can connect

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:38.340
to your users and get their
data from a cloud provider,

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.200
it's unclear what that
third-party provider

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.800
might do with the data.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.850
And this means that this
is one situation when

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:51.360
you have your social media data
being collected by an attacker,

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.277
by a third-party company.

00:07:53.277 --> 00:07:55.860
It's another thing entirely when
it's your corporate resources

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.030
that are being stolen.

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:02.250
And basically, in these
situations, your developers,

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.560
you just need to trick one
developer in your company

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.170
to install an application
on their phone

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.920
or on their browser,
and all of a sudden,

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.860
access to your corporate
resources could be granted.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.730
So we highly recommend that you
go to the Google main console

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:18.930
and turn off all
connected applications

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:22.650
for all scopes on all APIs
that are important to you,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.062
in particular for the Google
Cloud Platform, maybe others.

00:08:26.062 --> 00:08:28.020
And then at some point,
if you need to actually

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.730
have developers delegate
access to applications,

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:34.230
either first-party applications
or applications from vendors

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:36.330
you trust, you can vet
those applications,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.640
and you can use the
trusted app configuration

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.002
tools to whitelist individual
apps to have access

00:08:42.002 --> 00:08:42.960
to individual services.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:50.790
Now, I think I know what most
of you people are now thinking.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.760
You're probably thinking,
this is great, Cache,

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:55.830
but the thing that really
keeps we awake at night

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.520
are the attackers working from
their undersea lairs trying

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.070
to get access to my cloud.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:03.810
I want to tell you
that I, too, worry

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.800
about these kinds of situations.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:11.850
And we just announced the other
day our new set of security

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.780
features around protecting
access to your cloud resources

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:20.080
to what we call Secure
Contacts or Access Contacts.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.190
And the basic idea is
that I can configure it

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:26.370
in my Cloud Console the
set of networks or VPNs

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.650
that should have access
to my cloud resources

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:33.090
and essentially
mitigate and prevent

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:37.660
accesses to my resources from
outside of those networks.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.880
And the good thing about
this is not only does it

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.220
help mitigate against attacks
from deep sea, undersea lairs,

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:47.860
it also helps protect
from attackers in volcano

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:52.370
fortresses, or even moon bases.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.810
And the final thing I want to
tell you about organizations

00:09:55.810 --> 00:10:00.160
in Google Cloud is that they
give you an incredibly powerful

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.800
tool to organize and manage
your resources in a way that

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.050
makes sense to your business.

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:09.280
In particular, we find that
organizations are often

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.620
broken up into
individual product areas

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:15.580
and sometimes with different
types of deployments.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:17.980
For instance, internally
at Google, we often

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:22.447
speak about dev-level
environments,

00:10:22.447 --> 00:10:25.030
staging-level environments, and
production-level environments.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:26.654
And you can actually
model these things

00:10:26.654 --> 00:10:29.020
in folders and
different projects

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:31.390
and apply policies that
apply it down hierarchically

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.490
through the policy
hierarchy, essentially,

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.460
so that you can apply a
policy high in the policy

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:39.502
tree and to apply to all
resources underneath.

00:10:39.502 --> 00:10:40.960
Or for instance,
if something makes

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.690
sense only for a
specific product area,

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:47.290
you can apply a policy
to only the resources

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:49.484
within that product area.

00:10:49.484 --> 00:10:51.400
And with that, I'd like
to turn over to Sophia

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.470
to teach us about step two.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:54.700
SOPHIA YANG: All right.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.710
Thank you so much, Cache.

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:57.490
So now, great.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.077
You have set up
your organization,

00:10:59.077 --> 00:11:01.660
you have synced out a set of all
your organization identities,

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:04.090
or synced your
identity from on-prem.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:05.650
So now you realize
you have thousands

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.070
of employees on cloud.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:09.000
So how can you manage
that access of them?

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.650
How you make sure the right
person can perform their job,

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.140
like your developers
can go ahead,

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:15.670
deploy code in our codebase?

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:18.910
Also, you don't give everyone
a super power role, right?

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.910
And that is when you need to
set up your identity and access

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:26.420
management, or most
people call it IAM.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:28.420
So IAM am in a
nutshell is really

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:32.750
about giving you the control
to set who can perform which

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:35.590
actions on what resources.

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:37.860
So in an actual
example, for example,

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.300
I can say I want to grant
Cache the instance admin

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:43.040
role to my test project.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:44.990
That's going to allow
Cache to manipulate

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:46.670
my instances in
the test project,

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:48.710
right, create the et cetera.

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:51.560
I can also grant, say,
Sirui the storage admin

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.345
role in my project,
the same project.

00:11:53.345 --> 00:11:56.320
And Sirui can then
access my data

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:58.500
that I stored in a GCS bucket.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.430
Yet, Sirui wouldn't be
able to manipulate my VM

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:03.316
instance because he's
not instance admin.

00:12:03.316 --> 00:12:04.690
The same thing,
Cache wouldn't be

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:08.600
able to access the GCS bucket
unless they coordinate, right?

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:11.390
But the good thing, that's
what IAM can allow you,

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.919
a fine granular control
of who can do what.

00:12:14.919 --> 00:12:16.460
So then now you
probably can realize,

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.740
wow, I have thousands
of employees.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:21.916
The policy sounds like it
will look very complicated.

00:12:21.916 --> 00:12:23.300
Don't panic.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.510
No matter how and the result
might look complicated,

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.240
the steps you are taking
out of the set of the IAMs

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:30.230
are always the same.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:32.240
The policy language
is always the same.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:33.810
Which of the steps
you will follow

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:38.900
is first create a IAM policy and
then set the policy on a target

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:40.400
resource.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.720
So let's first look at what
an IAM policy looks like.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:46.730
Here is a JSON example.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:50.580
As you can tell, policy
contains a list of bindings.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:54.840
And so within each binding,
you define members and rules.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.910
And in this example,
all these members

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.680
are assigned with the
same image user role.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.080
So let's take a deeper
look at the members, which

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.510
is the circled part.

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:09.200
We allow three types of
identities at Google Cloud.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.440
One is the users, which
are the human users, two,

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:16.480
service accounts, who are the
identities of VMs, services,

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:18.740
so they are the
representation for robots,

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:21.450
pretty much, and the
groups, obviously

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.060
the group of multiple things.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:26.390
And you can put both human
users and the service accounts

00:13:26.390 --> 00:13:28.280
into one group.

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.980
And next, lastly,
we have the roles.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.790
The roles, or IAM
roles, which is really

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.090
a collection of permissions.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:42.000
So let's take a deeper
look at what roles can be.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:45.220
At Google, we support
three types of IAM roles.

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:47.830
The first two types
are primitive roles

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:50.050
and the predefined roles.

00:13:50.050 --> 00:13:53.590
There are three types within the
primitive roles, only three--

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:56.570
owner, editor, and the viewers.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.790
They each have very wide
access and very wide sets

00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:00.830
of permissions.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:03.320
They are meant to-- remember
we set a "get started" state?

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:06.476
Well, they are meant to
get you started quickly.

00:14:06.476 --> 00:14:07.850
But yet, they're
really not meant

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:09.770
for production for
exactly the same reason.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:10.610
They are too coarse.

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:14.330
They each have too wide
access of the permissions.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:16.530
So what should you use
in production system?

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:20.060
Predefined roles or sometimes
called the curated roles.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:20.760
What are they?

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:23.230
They are just really
fine-grained roles

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:26.090
that Google defined for you
that's specifically targeting

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:27.530
your specific use case.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.210
For example,
instance admin roles

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:32.360
are most likely mapped to your
developer functionalities.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:35.560
And the network
admin, probably you'll

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:37.980
give to your network
admin, as the name sounds,

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:40.220
to help you setup the
network's firewalls,

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:44.150
VPNs before a developer
comes to deploy code.

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:46.920
And our best practice
there is always

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:49.140
use the least privileged role.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:52.550
So do not grant any additional
permissions unless needed.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:54.600
That's for your
security purposes.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.150
And in order to better
implement this least

00:14:57.150 --> 00:14:59.190
privileged principle,
we also have

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:03.050
a third type of IAM roles,
which is custom IAM roles.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:06.540
We made this offering publicly
available earlier this year.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:08.380
As the name suggests,
it's allowing

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:10.470
you to customize
what permissions

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:13.620
you want to precisely
put into your role.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.610
So there are two
typical use cases

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.000
that you need to
use custom IAM role.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:22.140
One is the-- you might realize
our predefined roles are still

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:22.980
too limited.

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:25.230
Some customers
are saying, I have

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:27.810
to sometimes assign two
or three predefined roles

00:15:27.810 --> 00:15:30.290
to each identity
to do their job.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:34.050
And if I scale, it's a
little harder to manage.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:36.390
Many people on SRE used to
have both log viewer roles

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:39.069
and monitor viewer
roles in this example.

00:15:39.069 --> 00:15:40.860
So then you can create
your own custom role

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.590
to combine these
two roles together.

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:44.220
And then going
forward, you can just

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:46.480
assign this particular
custom role.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:48.780
Another use case
is the opposite.

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:51.230
Some customers find out our
predefined roles are still

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:53.130
too wide or it
doesn't necessarily

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.230
meet exactly their
business needs.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.510
A popular example will
be instance admin role.

00:15:57.510 --> 00:15:59.176
They want to give it
to their developer.

00:15:59.176 --> 00:16:02.430
They want everything about it
except the external IP usage

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:05.256
for exfiltration, infiltration.

00:16:05.256 --> 00:16:06.630
So they don't want
the developers

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:08.980
to be able to create a
VM with the external IP.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:12.420
So they would just easily remove
that particular permission

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:15.550
from the existing role and
create their own developer

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:16.150
admin role.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:23.680
And next, since we learn
about IAM policies, roles,

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:26.290
and identities, let's
talk about a few

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.150
best practices of
how to implement

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:30.460
those in the most secured way.

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:32.260
The first recommendation
we give to you

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:36.550
is always assigning permission
and access to groups, not

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:38.420
to individual users.

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:39.790
So why is that?

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:41.770
I know it's very natural
to think about, oh, I

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:44.710
have roles we should assign
to those users directly.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:48.010
But think about your
employees could move around

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.180
within a company.

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:51.480
If you're directly giving
them roles, permissions

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:53.320
on themselves,
after a while, you

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:55.450
might realize it's
harder to track,

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:59.320
to understand what permissions
this employee currently

00:16:59.320 --> 00:16:59.920
still need.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.470
Maybe he was in a different
group and needed something

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:03.850
else, but now he doesn't.

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:05.560
But after a while,
it's very hard

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:09.069
to analyze so that you
couldn't achieve the least

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:11.010
privileged principle, right?

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:15.069
But different, if you can assign
permissions directly onto group

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:17.800
and then add or remove
users from groups,

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.296
say, so one employee
leave a certain group,

00:17:20.296 --> 00:17:21.670
they get removed
from that group,

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:23.560
they automatically lose
all those permissions

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:24.550
they had before.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.379
It's much easier and
cleaner to manage

00:17:26.379 --> 00:17:28.770
the access for employees.

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:31.180
And at the same time,
service accounts

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:33.660
do not change roles and
move around as the humans.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:36.070
So we recommend you
directly set the policies

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:37.670
on service accounts.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:39.610
So that's the first
principle and also

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:43.680
best practice, grant
access to groups.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.080
Here's another one that we
have seen a lot of success

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.740
from our customers, which
is break-glass style.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:51.930
One of the approach is--

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.120
by the way, if you are not
familiar with this term,

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.450
think about fire distinguisher
on the wall, right?

00:17:57.450 --> 00:18:00.450
They are protected by the glass
so that if there is a fire,

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.880
you can access by
breaking the glass.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.680
But that's a very
invasive action.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.330
Takes a lot of additional
caution to do it.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.600
And by doing so,
you're preventing

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.020
a lot of accidental errors.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.680
So one of the styles to
implement this break-glass

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:23.640
is to assign human users nothing
but the project IAM admin role.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:27.030
What this particular role only
allows is set permissions,

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:27.790
set policies.

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:29.010
That's it.

00:18:29.010 --> 00:18:31.446
So if you give your on-call
this particular role,

00:18:31.446 --> 00:18:33.070
they wouldn't be able
to just go ahead,

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:36.000
stop your VM in production
system, because they can't.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:37.650
They don't have the permission.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:40.480
But yet, as we said, it's
called a break glass.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.750
So if emergency arises, if
there is an urgent situation,

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:46.680
they do have to go ahead and
manipulate their VM instance.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:50.040
They can use their
project IAM admin role

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.475
to grant themselves the
instance admin role.

00:18:52.475 --> 00:18:53.850
And from there,
they can go ahead

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:55.680
and manipulate the VM instance.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:58.560
But then this extra layer of
caution and actual requirements

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:01.970
can help you prevent a
lot of accidental actions.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:05.270
And by the way, all these change
of policies, setting policies,

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:08.220
are in the auto-log, so you
can go back to trace that.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.630
And with that, I'm
sure many of you

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:14.680
already have questions,
want to know more

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:15.996
about service accounts.

00:19:15.996 --> 00:19:17.620
So let's invite Cache
back to tell more

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:20.320
about service accounts.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:21.320
CACHE MCWHERTER: Thanks.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:26.960
As most of you guys
know, service accounts

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:30.420
are quite fun toys to play with.

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:33.650
They tirelessly execute
all of our actions for us

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:37.190
when we're not there
at the keyboard.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:42.080
They run our jobs, they run our
services, they never get paid,

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:45.530
and yet they're
always there for us.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:47.530
But sometimes they're
hard to understand.

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:49.120
And so I'd like
to first help you

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:51.310
get to know what the service
accounts that we offer

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:53.601
look like, and feel like,
and how to differentiate them

00:19:53.601 --> 00:19:56.770
and make sense of them
as you encounter them.

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:00.070
This is a common cause
of confusion and errors

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:03.490
that we see when we talk
to our external customers.

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:07.420
This is an IAM policy page that
you see in the Google Cloud

00:20:07.420 --> 00:20:09.790
Developer console.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:12.480
This is the landing page
when you get to IAM.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:15.590
As you can see,
this IAM indicates

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:17.380
this is an IAM policy.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.110
This is the policy
that grants access

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:22.830
to this project and all the
resources contained within.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:24.870
As Sophia was
indicating earlier,

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:26.610
policy is essentially
just a mapping

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:30.030
of the sets of permissions
to a set of identities.

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:35.280
So for instance, this one
grants me owner on this project.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.650
This is a service account.

00:20:37.650 --> 00:20:40.050
You can tend to identify
them by the fact

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:42.070
that they end in
gserviceaccount.com email

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:43.380
addresses.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.060
The name here
indicates what it is.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:46.920
It's a service account
that was created

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:50.430
by default by the system to
get me started using the cloud.

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.950
It has editor access,
which is fairly powerful.

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.950
And clearly this is not a
production project, right?

00:20:55.950 --> 00:20:58.020
Everything that we told
you before about securing

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:01.559
your projects was not done
here, and so you can imagine

00:21:01.559 --> 00:21:02.850
what that might look like here.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:05.190
You've taken
cache@google.com off.

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.740
You've granted him
project IAM admin.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.450
You've taken--
well, you actually

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:13.410
granted him project IAM
admin through his presence

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.180
on a team called Team David.

00:21:16.180 --> 00:21:18.180
The service account
at the top has

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:21.510
been granted Cloud Datastore
user because all he is doing

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:24.259
is accessing Datastore,
for instance.

00:21:24.259 --> 00:21:26.550
And so this makes sure that
if that job is compromised,

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:30.060
the impact and blast radius
is limited to the Datastore

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:32.490
and not, for instance,
my VMs or other things.

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:38.220
These other service
accounts here in the middle

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:41.010
are a common cause of
confusion for our customers.

00:21:41.010 --> 00:21:44.712
They represent the
identities of services

00:21:44.712 --> 00:21:49.380
activated and essentially
purchased from Google.

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:51.060
I didn't create them,
I don't own them,

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.470
and I don't manage them.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:55.520
They're owned and managed by
Google and protected for you.

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:59.640
They're kept securely under lock
and key for only our production

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:01.560
services to access.

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:05.550
They've been granted particular
API access to your resources

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:06.660
in this project.

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:08.610
And they specifically
grant access

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:12.930
to only the types of
data and operations

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:15.030
that are in line
with the business

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:17.160
requirements of those services.

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:21.204
And we basically review
every one of those security

00:22:21.204 --> 00:22:22.620
grants-- every one
of those grants

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:24.930
before we allow them to be
granted to your projects

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:28.320
to make sure that they
actually make logical sense

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:31.560
and meet your expectations.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:33.390
If I wanted to look at
the service accounts

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:37.470
that I own and I manage, you
can flip to the service account

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:38.030
tab.

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:40.530
And here, you can see that I
have two service accounts, one,

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:42.030
the default one
that we told you was

00:22:42.030 --> 00:22:45.000
created earlier by the system
for me to get me started.

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:49.420
But I also created another one
called My Web FE, which I am

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:51.480
using to run my web front end.

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:58.080
There are two ways that you
can use these service accounts.

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:00.540
The first way is
to use what we call

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:03.030
Google-Managed Credentials,
which essentially means

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.970
that you don't have
to worry about any

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.580
of the key management
or the rotation story.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:11.670
We keep the service
accounts secure for you.

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:14.280
It's a tireless task, but
somebody's got to do it.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:16.030
They don't pay me
enough to do that,

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:20.460
but we make sure that they
always get rotated for you.

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:22.230
And they're kept secure.

00:23:22.230 --> 00:23:25.830
The basic idea here is
that I can ask a service,

00:23:25.830 --> 00:23:28.560
like Google Compute, or Google
App Engine, or Google Cloud

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:32.270
Functions to use a
service account here.

00:23:32.270 --> 00:23:34.290
I'm asking, for
instance, Compute Service

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:39.070
to create an instance bound to
my front end service account.

00:23:39.070 --> 00:23:41.461
And what happens
is an actAs check--

00:23:41.461 --> 00:23:43.710
a permission called actAs
will be checked to make sure

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:45.751
that I have permission to
use the service account

00:23:45.751 --> 00:23:47.130
and bind it to a job.

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:49.530
And then once I've
been granted access,

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.380
that job will run
continuously without me being

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:54.270
there as that service account.

00:23:54.270 --> 00:23:57.660
And that VM, the
code inside the VM,

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.042
can go and access
data commiserate

00:24:00.042 --> 00:24:01.500
with the permissions
that have been

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:04.560
granted to the service account.

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.510
The other way of
using service accounts

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:08.760
is to download a
service account key.

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:11.479
And this is a less
secure, we think,

00:24:11.479 --> 00:24:13.020
way of managing your
service accounts

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:14.610
and using your service
accounts, because you

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:16.401
have to take responsibility
for making sure

00:24:16.401 --> 00:24:17.580
that key doesn't leak.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.579
And you have to take
responsibility for rotating

00:24:19.579 --> 00:24:20.880
that key to keep it secure.

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:23.010
It's predominantly
there for you to use

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:25.811
when connecting your on-premise
systems to Google Cloud

00:24:25.811 --> 00:24:26.310
Platform.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:30.210
For instance, if you needed to
do database backup, or computer

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:33.442
backup, or something like
this to Cloud Storage,

00:24:33.442 --> 00:24:35.400
you could use a service
account key to do that.

00:24:39.226 --> 00:24:41.350
Many of you are Google
Compute customers, I'm sure.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:44.100
And you've seen in the user
interfaces, and the APIs,

00:24:44.100 --> 00:24:47.080
and the command lines
that, when you create VMs,

00:24:47.080 --> 00:24:50.410
we often ask you for a set of
access control scopes, which

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:52.750
are scopes when
you're creating a VM.

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:56.270
We call these VM lock
scopes, essentially.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:59.770
They're a legacy feature
of Compute from the days

00:24:59.770 --> 00:25:01.900
before Google IAM
came on the scene.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:03.870
They caused some confusion
to our customers.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:06.230
And so I'd like to clarify
some of the usage here.

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:09.300
They used to be used for some
course-grained access control,

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:11.920
but they're not really
good for that these days.

00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:14.060
So we highly recommend
that, in general,

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:16.720
when you see these things,
you should close your eyes

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:19.260
and don't use them
for access control.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.310
Rely on IAM for access control.

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:25.910
And instead, give the VM
a fairly high-level scope,

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:28.930
such as Google Cloud
Platform or full access

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:31.040
depending on the user
interface you're using.

00:25:31.040 --> 00:25:33.280
And if you need to call
other APIs, like Calendar,

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:38.390
or Maps, or something, you can
grant those scopes as well.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:40.531
And I think Sophia has
something more to tell you

00:25:40.531 --> 00:25:41.780
about managing your resources.

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:42.790
SOPHIA YANG: Yep.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:43.290
All right.

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:44.580
Thank you, Cache.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:45.080
Yeah.

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:47.840
So now we have got our
employee imported here

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:51.110
in the Google Cloud and a set
of IAM policies onto them,

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:53.730
also set up additional
IAM permissions

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:55.490
onto the service accounts.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:56.950
So now what's left to do?

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:59.395
You are going to come here
to create a VM instance,

00:25:59.395 --> 00:26:01.300
right, creating disks as images.

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:03.410
How you make sure you
manage the access to those,

00:26:03.410 --> 00:26:04.920
to secure those resources?

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:06.560
And that's what this
section is we're

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:08.900
going to be talking about.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:11.510
So let's have a quick review
of what Cache showed earlier

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:13.530
about our policy hierarchies.

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:17.690
At Google, we offer four levels
of hierarchies-- organization,

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:20.600
folders, projects,
and the resources.

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:24.650
So you can set up permissions,
policies on each of a level,

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:28.070
and any level down below will
automatically inherit it.

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:32.270
So for example, if I want to
give architect the image user

00:26:32.270 --> 00:26:35.180
role for all of the
team A, so then I

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:37.400
can go ahead and set that
policy on the folder level,

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:40.310
team A. Then this architect
will automatically

00:26:40.310 --> 00:26:45.080
become the image user role for
all the project A, project two,

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:48.840
and et cetera, any
projects under the folder.

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:51.350
So this give you a lot
of flexibility to use.

00:26:51.350 --> 00:26:53.960
But sometimes you might
want the opposite.

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:56.210
You actually want to run
into the situation you only

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.370
want to give access to
a particular resource

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:00.870
instead of grant
it a higher level.

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:02.370
So what would you do there?

00:27:02.370 --> 00:27:04.760
You can set the policies
onto the individual resource,

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:07.534
like VMs, disks.

00:27:07.534 --> 00:27:09.950
But probably, many people are
familiar with Compute Engine

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:10.490
IAM.

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:13.292
You might ask me, I didn't know
that you were able to do so.

00:27:13.292 --> 00:27:14.750
I thought, at the
lowest level, you

00:27:14.750 --> 00:27:17.370
can't set a policy with
the projects level.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.890
And you are right
until this week.

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.100
We're actually
very excited to let

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:26.390
you know that we are going
to support resource-level IAM

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:28.340
in beta very soon for you guys.

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:31.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:31.220 --> 00:27:33.440
Highly anticipated
feature, it seems like.

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:33.980
Great.

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:36.520
And what it allows
you to do is exactly

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:39.080
to set IAM policies on
individual resource,

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:41.180
like VMs, disks.

00:27:41.180 --> 00:27:44.760
This totally enables a
whole new set of use cases.

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:47.780
For example, a common use case
is, within an organization,

00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:49.940
people often like
to share images

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:52.280
with other developers
or different users.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:54.620
Yet, without the
resource-level IAM,

00:27:54.620 --> 00:27:57.260
they have to give the image
user role for the whole project

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:58.086
or above, right?

00:27:58.086 --> 00:27:59.960
But sometimes you do
want to give them access

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:03.260
to all the image, just
the production imagery.

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:06.080
Before resource-level
IAM, what you had to do

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:08.200
was create a separate
project, and only

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:09.950
put that particular
image in that project,

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:13.210
and then grant policies
onto the project level.

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:16.670
That's suboptimal and a lot
of management overhead, right?

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:19.080
So was this
resource-level IAM, you

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.380
can simply just
grant the IAM policy

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:23.130
onto the particular image.

00:28:23.130 --> 00:28:26.240
In this example here, I
want to grant the beta test

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:29.990
group the access to the
particular beta image.

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:34.790
And just by simple one line of
code, you can achieve that now.

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:36.920
So here is a real-world
example of sharing images,

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:39.770
as we were talking about
this scenario, right?

00:28:39.770 --> 00:28:42.290
So now you can create
one single image project

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:44.945
and then put in your
production image

00:28:44.945 --> 00:28:47.990
together with our beta testing
image in the same project,

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:50.800
and only grant the developers
access to the production image

00:28:50.800 --> 00:28:53.390
so to make sure everyone uses
the most supported and verified

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:56.240
version, and giving
the beta testers access

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:58.790
to the beta testing images.

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:00.830
So now you probably
start thinking,

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:04.070
yeah, I can set IAM policies
on each beta testing image

00:29:04.070 --> 00:29:06.050
to give the beta testers.

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:09.310
But yet, if I have many
of the beta testing image,

00:29:09.310 --> 00:29:13.960
I have to set the policy on
individual image every time.

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:15.860
Would that be a little bit--

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:17.450
a lot of work to do, right?

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:19.210
And we have thought
about that for you.

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:22.220
So if you want to have another
way to manage your resources

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:25.025
access at scale, they
will be happy to find out

00:29:25.025 --> 00:29:28.480
another beta feature that's
coming soon to you, which

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:32.290
is name Prefix IAM condition.

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:36.340
What this allows you to do is
exactly manage your resources

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:38.967
under the condition
based on name Prefix.

00:29:38.967 --> 00:29:41.050
So let's backtrack a little
bit to talk about what

00:29:41.050 --> 00:29:43.120
is conditional IAM first.

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:44.810
As I mentioned
earlier, IAM's allowing

00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:48.100
you to define who can do what
on what resources, right?

00:29:48.100 --> 00:29:49.750
And now conditional
IAM giving you

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:52.370
additional power, which
is adding a condition now

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:54.430
to the IAM access.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:57.280
So we wouldn't grant access
unless the condition is also

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:58.240
met.

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:00.170
In this name Prefix
Condition, we

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:04.280
would say, for example, you can
grant beta testers the image

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:08.110
user role on a project, but
only if the image, the name,

00:30:08.110 --> 00:30:09.730
is starting with beta.

00:30:09.730 --> 00:30:11.980
That way you can achieve
what I just mentioned.

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:14.770
By setting one policy,
the beta testing groups

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:19.150
will have access to all the
images starting with name beta.

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:20.650
And that's even
including the images

00:30:20.650 --> 00:30:22.030
that you have yet to create.

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:25.730
As long as you created them
under the beta underscore name,

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:30.670
all your beta testers will
have access to that image.

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:32.560
Another common
use case customers

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:36.550
ask for that you can utilize
this name Prefix Condition is

00:30:36.550 --> 00:30:39.820
to build this so-called
Developer Playground.

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:42.326
Many companies, when they
have new developers join in,

00:30:42.326 --> 00:30:44.200
they like to put them
all in the same project

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:46.810
to test out, to build
their prototypes,

00:30:46.810 --> 00:30:49.125
because creating each one
on individual projects

00:30:49.125 --> 00:30:51.230
is a lot of overhead.

00:30:51.230 --> 00:30:55.270
Yet, they don't want every
developer to accidentally stomp

00:30:55.270 --> 00:30:57.160
upon other people's resource.

00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:00.490
So setting up the name
Prefix Condition to base

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:03.060
on each developer's
email address

00:31:03.060 --> 00:31:06.430
solves the situation so that
each developer will still

00:31:06.430 --> 00:31:08.500
only have access
to the resources

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:10.900
starting with their
names, and yet they

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:13.030
can still be enjoy,
within the same project,

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:19.420
the convenience and also the
high network performance.

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:22.150
More excitingly, you will
find out conditional IAM

00:31:22.150 --> 00:31:24.775
not only allow allowing to do
name Prefix Basic Condition.

00:31:24.775 --> 00:31:27.730
We have two more type of
conditions we support,

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:31.570
which is access level and
the date time condition.

00:31:31.570 --> 00:31:34.720
Access level utilizes the
security context thing

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:36.770
that Cache mentioned earlier--

00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:41.020
sorry-- that we will look at
the role attributes coming from

00:31:41.020 --> 00:31:43.990
the request, and
then to decide--

00:31:43.990 --> 00:31:46.070
even, for example, the
instance admin, say,

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:48.100
you can manipulate
an instance, right

00:31:48.100 --> 00:31:51.580
but you can say any
actions to manipulate

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:53.560
the instance for a
production project,

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:57.160
this request needs to come
in from my corp network.

00:31:57.160 --> 00:32:00.400
That way even that instance
admin sitting in their home,

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:02.990
trying to do some work, the
request will be rejected,

00:32:02.990 --> 00:32:06.480
so giving you another layer
for security and protection.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:09.425
Date time condition, just
as the name suggests,

00:32:09.425 --> 00:32:12.350
it allows you to get a
timer around your condition

00:32:12.350 --> 00:32:15.950
so that you can say,
grant a powerful access

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:20.060
to your on-call SREs but only
during their on-call hours.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.260
Before this, you had to
grant access and then

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:25.160
remove access manually at the
end of their on-call time.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:28.880
But now you can just simply
set up a time-based condition.

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:31.500
And it can be recurring as well.

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:35.100
So we can help you manage that.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:35.830
All right.

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:39.040
So we talk a lot about
IAMs, conditional IAMs,

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:42.320
resource IAMs to help you
better manage our resources.

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:44.050
Let's talk about
another dimension

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:46.410
and go to how to
manage the access

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:49.720
for your organization, which
is the famous organizational

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:52.320
policy.

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:56.220
Organization policies are
meant to work together side

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:58.140
by side with IAM policies.

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:01.770
But there's a key difference,
which is, org policies,

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:05.430
the enforcement will be there
regardless of IAM access.

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:07.980
So for example, if you
utilize-- the first example

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:10.680
shows no external
IP or org policy.

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:14.280
You can set that on your project
folder or organization level

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:17.550
saying, oh, in my
organization, for example,

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:21.080
I don't allow VMs to be
created with an external IP.

00:33:21.080 --> 00:33:22.580
Then even the person
that's supposed

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:24.807
to have that IAM permission,
like computer admins,

00:33:24.807 --> 00:33:25.890
wouldn't be able to do so.

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:28.460
So this helps prevent
a lot of human errors.

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:30.930
Again, it's an organizational
policy enforced

00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:33.210
at whatever level
you are granted.

00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:35.680
And similar as IAM
policy, any level

00:33:35.680 --> 00:33:38.430
below will automatically
inherit that.

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:41.010
And Compute Engine will have
a bunch of very useful org

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:44.630
policies that you can read
about, like trusted image,

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:47.392
disable serial-port
access, et cetera.

00:33:47.392 --> 00:33:49.725
There are, in particular, two
more organization policies

00:33:49.725 --> 00:33:50.940
that we'd like to call out.

00:33:50.940 --> 00:33:53.160
And we really think you
should know about them.

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:55.690
And we will highly recommend
you to enable them.

00:33:55.690 --> 00:33:57.270
The first one is
domain-restricted

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:58.990
sharing organization policy.

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:01.440
What this is allowing
you to restrict,

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:05.160
the resources wouldn't be
shared outside the organization.

00:34:05.160 --> 00:34:08.659
We have seen mistakes done
before from a customer

00:34:08.659 --> 00:34:10.701
that, for example, their
developer will go home,

00:34:10.701 --> 00:34:12.159
and they're trying
to do some work,

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:16.615
but they accidentally share
this image with their IP.

00:34:16.615 --> 00:34:19.690
It was their own Gmail account,
and there is information leak

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:20.190
there.

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:21.870
With this organizational
policy, you

00:34:21.870 --> 00:34:24.750
can secure that resources
wouldn't be shared to anywhere

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.130
outside your organization.

00:34:27.130 --> 00:34:30.210
The next one, Disable Service
Account Keys, this [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:31.980
back what Cache
mentioned earlier.

00:34:31.980 --> 00:34:33.090
We want to have a Google--

00:34:33.090 --> 00:34:35.370
we recommend you use Google
to manage the key instead

00:34:35.370 --> 00:34:37.374
of user-generated credentials.

00:34:37.374 --> 00:34:38.790
And if you enable
this org policy,

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:42.260
it will prevent exactly
user-managed generated key

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:44.429
so that you wouldn't
have long-living keys

00:34:44.429 --> 00:34:46.955
that developers
hold and that might

00:34:46.955 --> 00:34:48.844
expose additional risk
to your organization,

00:34:48.844 --> 00:34:51.100
to your environment.

00:34:51.100 --> 00:34:53.610
And we have a bunch more
organization policies

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:55.216
we can introduce for you.

00:34:55.216 --> 00:34:57.090
Yet, you can read them
online probably better

00:34:57.090 --> 00:34:59.004
than what I can say
each about them.

00:34:59.004 --> 00:35:00.420
But I think, more
interesting, you

00:35:00.420 --> 00:35:03.360
will be interested to find out
how a real customer, like Two

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:06.350
Sigma, set up and utilize these
IAM policies and organization

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:08.290
policies to build
their environment.

00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:10.920
So we're happy to invite
Nick, the head of Cloud

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:14.665
Security at Two Sigma, to
tell us about your story.

00:35:14.665 --> 00:35:15.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:16.150
Welcome.

00:35:21.100 --> 00:35:23.242
NICK ARVANITIS:
Thank you, Sophia.

00:35:23.242 --> 00:35:25.710
Yeah, we're also very
excited about IAM conditions

00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:27.850
and conditional roles.

00:35:27.850 --> 00:35:29.100
My name's Nick.

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:30.690
Two Sigma-- I'll
talk a little bit

00:35:30.690 --> 00:35:34.980
about our business to put our
threat model into context.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:37.620
We're a technology-driven,
data-driven, quantitative

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:38.829
investment manager.

00:35:38.829 --> 00:35:40.620
Really, what that means
is we use computers

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:44.400
to analyze a ton of data
sets and try to make market

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:46.410
decisions based off that.

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:49.500
We're obviously very worried
about external attackers,

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:51.420
though we have a very
small external profile.

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:54.630
So although we're worried
about volcano fortresses,

00:35:54.630 --> 00:35:57.720
we're also worried about
our internal users.

00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:00.089
And that's not to say that
we don't trust our people.

00:36:00.089 --> 00:36:02.130
There's some extent to
which we don't trust them,

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:03.600
but we do trust them.

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:05.800
But if you follow
security and compromises,

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:09.330
you know that sophisticated
attackers are targeting people

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:11.376
with legitimate access to data.

00:36:11.376 --> 00:36:12.750
And we have some
sort of friction

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.060
whereby we need our
people to have access

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:17.700
to the data and the
resources they need in order

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:21.180
to do their jobs, but then also
mitigate the risk of mistakes

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:24.340
or intentional compromises.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:25.890
So we want to protect our IP.

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:28.680
And one of the big use cases
we have for Compute Engine

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:31.380
is really as an extension
of our data center.

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:36.210
We run a lot of trading
simulations or model tests

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:39.450
and simulations that
use tons of compute.

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:41.680
And that's not exposed
to the internet at all.

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:43.750
It's in a hybrid-type
environment.

00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:46.860
So things like what
Sophia mentioned is we

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:49.230
don't want external IPs
in that environment.

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:52.230
Even though there's no internet
access, mistakes can happen.

00:36:52.230 --> 00:36:55.200
And we believe very strongly
in defense in depth.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:58.980
You don't want to be one error
or one failure of a control,

00:36:58.980 --> 00:37:01.320
or one compromise
away from being

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:03.480
on the front page of the
papers or, in our case,

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:04.860
potentially out of business.

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:07.280
So we apply
organization policies

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:09.470
as a really cheap and
easy to implement control,

00:37:09.470 --> 00:37:12.210
but with really
powerful implications,

00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:13.684
like no external IPs.

00:37:13.684 --> 00:37:15.600
Something else that's
really interesting to us

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:17.940
is, a really useful
feature, the ability

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:20.280
to connect to a compute
instance as serial console

00:37:20.280 --> 00:37:23.730
if you have a network
outage and troubleshoot it.

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:26.220
Our issue is that that
doesn't take into account

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:28.890
any of our file policies
or network configurations.

00:37:28.890 --> 00:37:32.670
So we hate that
and we turn it off.

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:35.190
You can tackle this,
again, via IAM.

00:37:35.190 --> 00:37:38.010
But one of our use cases,
again, enabling velocity

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:40.170
for our teams, is
my partner team

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:42.180
that runs this environment
would really hate it

00:37:42.180 --> 00:37:44.940
if I didn't allow them the
ability to configure metadata

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:47.850
on VM instances where you
can turn this on or off.

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:49.260
They need that
for bootstrapping.

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:52.650
So a safe compromise is to
use this organization policy

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:54.210
to enforce that
control and then be

00:37:54.210 --> 00:37:57.000
able to be more lenient on IAM.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:37:59.520
Again, we really want to
be restrictive about what

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:00.420
images we use.

00:38:00.420 --> 00:38:02.100
We have a lot of
custom configuration

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:03.630
that goes into those.

00:38:03.630 --> 00:38:06.910
So we have-- as suggested
in the best practices,

00:38:06.910 --> 00:38:09.900
we have a single project where
our trusted image is built,

00:38:09.900 --> 00:38:12.900
and then we use an org policy
to configure our environments

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:14.940
that only images
from that environment

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.740
can be booted up and used.

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:20.190
The flip side of that is also
obviously really important.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:23.730
We, too, have a developer
sandbox, like everybody else.

00:38:23.730 --> 00:38:26.070
And we don't want, in
that environment that's

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:29.700
wide open and connected to
the internet, our images that

00:38:29.700 --> 00:38:33.030
are baked with our IP
and, in other cases,

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:36.640
sensitive configuration
details to be used at all.

00:38:36.640 --> 00:38:38.465
So we use another
org policy for that.

00:38:38.465 --> 00:38:39.990
And finally, as
Sophia mentioned,

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:42.840
domain-restricted sharing
really is a no-brainer for us.

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:45.570
It's super easy to implement.

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:48.500
And these things really
provide us a lot of access.

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:50.010
Again, other best
practices, this

00:38:50.010 --> 00:38:51.420
is really defense in depth.

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:54.960
We rely very strongly on
using custom IAM roles only.

00:38:54.960 --> 00:38:56.984
We tailor our roles
based on least privilege.

00:38:56.984 --> 00:38:59.400
And we use a whole host of
other services for our security

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:00.050
posture.

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:03.000
But these offerings and the ones
that have been discussed today

00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:04.680
are really essential
for us in terms

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:07.890
of enabling our business without
adding unnecessary friction

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:12.060
either to our security team
or to our client teams.

00:39:12.060 --> 00:39:14.860
So with that, I'll hand
to Sirui to wrap up

00:39:14.860 --> 00:39:19.485
with the final section of how
to secure your environment.

00:39:19.485 --> 00:39:22.450
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:24.890
SIRUI SUN: Thank you, Nick.

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:25.800
So hi, everyone.

00:39:25.800 --> 00:39:26.508
My name is Sirui.

00:39:26.508 --> 00:39:28.444
I'm another product
manager on the GCE team.

00:39:28.444 --> 00:39:29.860
And actually, first
of all, I want

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:32.790
to say thank you so much, Nick,
for talking with us today.

00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:34.640
It's been an absolute
blast and a privilege

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:37.550
to be able to work with
you guys to really secure

00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:39.020
your incredibly valuable IP.

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:40.800
So thank you again.

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:42.850
Let's hear another round
for Nick, Two Sigma.

00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:45.591
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:45.981 --> 00:39:46.480
Great.

00:39:46.480 --> 00:39:50.440
So I'm here to take us home and
talk about the final step here

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:52.330
in our six steps best practices.

00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:55.480
And I'm here to talk to you
guys about platform transparency

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:57.020
and data integrity.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:39:59.740
So you may be wondering,
right, what does that mean?

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:02.560
So to boil it down,
here at Google Cloud,

00:40:02.560 --> 00:40:05.090
your trust is our
top priority, right?

00:40:05.090 --> 00:40:08.290
We understand that many of
you, many of our customers,

00:40:08.290 --> 00:40:11.650
really rely on Google Cloud to
run their businesses, right?

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:14.020
And we know that wouldn't
be possible without having

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:16.489
deep trust in how we
administer our platform.

00:40:16.489 --> 00:40:19.030
So the way I think about this
is, when we talk about platform

00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:21.070
transparency here,
what we really mean

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:24.070
is, what are the tools and what
is the information that we're

00:40:24.070 --> 00:40:27.700
giving you guys as our
customers to really have faith,

00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:29.950
and trust, and to really
build that trust in how we're

00:40:29.950 --> 00:40:31.760
administering our
platform, right?

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:34.150
And as we'll see that takes
a few different forms.

00:40:34.150 --> 00:40:35.650
The first thing I
want to talk about

00:40:35.650 --> 00:40:38.030
is how your data is
encrypted, right?

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:39.760
And so before I go
any further, I just

00:40:39.760 --> 00:40:41.830
want to say that,
at Google Cloud,

00:40:41.830 --> 00:40:44.390
regardless of how you
store your data with us,

00:40:44.390 --> 00:40:46.390
we are going to be
encrypting your data

00:40:46.390 --> 00:40:48.220
at rest full stop, rihgt?

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:50.840
And in the spirit of
transparency-- thank you,

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:51.660
Cache--

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:54.520
in the spirit of transparency,
you can go online.

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:57.430
If you Google, for example,
Google Cloud Security white

00:40:57.430 --> 00:41:01.660
paper, we have a lot of white
papers and other resources

00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:04.240
for y'all so that you can
read about our security best

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:07.592
practices in general, how we
do that encryption at rest,

00:41:07.592 --> 00:41:08.300
things like that.

00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:09.700
So I would highly
encourage all of you,

00:41:09.700 --> 00:41:12.116
if you are interested, to go
take a look at that following

00:41:12.116 --> 00:41:13.360
this presentation.

00:41:13.360 --> 00:41:15.790
What I'm talking about
here is the options

00:41:15.790 --> 00:41:19.000
that we give you all in GCE,
in Google Compute Engine,

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:22.954
for how we actually do that
encryption at rest, right?

00:41:22.954 --> 00:41:25.120
And so here you see we have
three different options.

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:26.650
So I go left to right.

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:29.050
And they fall in this
spectrum of automation,

00:41:29.050 --> 00:41:31.870
so how much control we
give versus how automated

00:41:31.870 --> 00:41:32.995
the encryption actually is.

00:41:32.995 --> 00:41:35.494
So we'll start with the one on
the left, which is encryption

00:41:35.494 --> 00:41:36.280
by default, right?

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:38.770
So if you go into Google
Cloud Compute Engine,

00:41:38.770 --> 00:41:41.320
you spin up a disk, you spin
up an image or a snapshot,

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:43.810
and you just have all
the default parameters,

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:46.990
we will encrypt that for
you automatically at rest,

00:41:46.990 --> 00:41:50.080
no questions asked, using
our world-class encryption

00:41:50.080 --> 00:41:51.681
methodologies, right?

00:41:51.681 --> 00:41:53.180
So you don't have
to worry about it.

00:41:53.180 --> 00:41:55.840
You just go ahead and spin
it up, encrypted at rest.

00:41:55.840 --> 00:41:58.030
Now, we know that some
of you, as our customers,

00:41:58.030 --> 00:42:00.070
you maybe work in
industries, like banking,

00:42:00.070 --> 00:42:02.740
or financial services, or
consulting where you're

00:42:02.740 --> 00:42:05.290
expected or you're
legally obligated to have

00:42:05.290 --> 00:42:09.400
some more rigor or more control
in how your data is protected

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:10.360
at rest, right?

00:42:10.360 --> 00:42:13.600
And so as a result of that, we
give you some more options here

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:16.150
to give you more control over
how your data is actually

00:42:16.150 --> 00:42:16.990
encrypted.

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:19.340
So I'll talk about
that middle one next.

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:23.650
And this is a thing that we
call customer-managed encryption

00:42:23.650 --> 00:42:24.460
keys.

00:42:24.460 --> 00:42:26.560
And so here what
you do is, right,

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:30.190
you create a key in
another GCP service

00:42:30.190 --> 00:42:32.772
called Cloud Key
Management Service.

00:42:32.772 --> 00:42:33.730
So you create that key.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:35.605
And then when you create
a disk, or an image,

00:42:35.605 --> 00:42:39.112
or a snapshot in GCE, what we
go ahead and do is we ask you,

00:42:39.112 --> 00:42:40.570
hey, which one of
these keys do you

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:43.690
want to use to actually
protect this content, right?

00:42:43.690 --> 00:42:47.050
And so here you are deferring
the creation and the storage

00:42:47.050 --> 00:42:49.660
of the keys to GCP,
but you're still

00:42:49.660 --> 00:42:52.000
in full control of the
lifecycle of that key.

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:53.070
You can revoke access.

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:54.250
You can disable the key.

00:42:54.250 --> 00:42:56.681
You can delete it,
et cetera, right?

00:42:56.681 --> 00:42:58.430
And then finally, on
the very right there,

00:42:58.430 --> 00:43:00.013
we have another
option called customer

00:43:00.013 --> 00:43:01.730
supplied encryption keys.

00:43:01.730 --> 00:43:03.140
And so in this
case, what happens

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:05.157
is we just ask you for the key.

00:43:05.157 --> 00:43:06.740
And Google Cloud is
actually deferring

00:43:06.740 --> 00:43:09.170
the creation and storage of
the key to you, the customer,

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:09.670
right?

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:12.207
So this gives you full control.

00:43:12.207 --> 00:43:13.790
When you create the
disk, for example,

00:43:13.790 --> 00:43:14.972
we ask you for the key.

00:43:14.972 --> 00:43:16.430
We obviously don't
store it, but we

00:43:16.430 --> 00:43:17.990
use it to encrypt your content.

00:43:17.990 --> 00:43:21.050
And the next time that disk
is used, say a VM startup,

00:43:21.050 --> 00:43:23.476
we go ahead and ask you
for that key again, right?

00:43:23.476 --> 00:43:24.475
So this is full control.

00:43:24.475 --> 00:43:26.280
And this is really
popular, again,

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:30.820
in some of those verticals
like banking, ok?

00:43:30.820 --> 00:43:33.130
Switching gears a little--
actually, just kidding.

00:43:33.130 --> 00:43:37.300
Continuing on, like
most features in GCE,

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:40.290
these controls are
available to you in the UI,

00:43:40.290 --> 00:43:42.040
in the command
line, or in the API.

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:43.510
So here I'm going
to show you, just

00:43:43.510 --> 00:43:45.009
for simplicity's
sake, what it looks

00:43:45.009 --> 00:43:48.070
like in the UI, what these three
options look like in the UI.

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:51.080
So here, this is the
VM Creation Page.

00:43:51.080 --> 00:43:53.080
This is, when you
create a VM, we

00:43:53.080 --> 00:43:55.060
ask you to create to
boot disk, of course.

00:43:55.060 --> 00:43:58.060
And you can actually select
either the automatic option

00:43:58.060 --> 00:44:00.370
on the far left where no
questions asked, we just

00:44:00.370 --> 00:44:01.991
go ahead and do it for you.

00:44:01.991 --> 00:44:03.490
In the middle,
you'll see the option

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:06.950
to specify a key in
Key Management Service.

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:09.376
So that dropdown is a set
of keys you have there.

00:44:09.376 --> 00:44:10.750
And then on the
far right, that's

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:12.166
the customer-supplied
option where

00:44:12.166 --> 00:44:14.650
we ask you what the key is.

00:44:14.650 --> 00:44:17.260
You input it in that
box and you go ahead.

00:44:17.260 --> 00:44:20.590
So the take away here
really is that, however you

00:44:20.590 --> 00:44:23.240
want to manage how your data
is encrypted at rest, we may

00:44:23.240 --> 00:44:26.170
we make it really easy
for you to get started

00:44:26.170 --> 00:44:28.850
and to get going with that.

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:31.181
So switching gears a little
bit, another question

00:44:31.181 --> 00:44:33.430
that we get asked at Google
Cloud a lot is, well, hey,

00:44:33.430 --> 00:44:35.890
you guys are
administering our data.

00:44:35.890 --> 00:44:37.660
How much access do
you have to it, right?

00:44:37.660 --> 00:44:39.350
Are you reading it constantly?

00:44:39.350 --> 00:44:42.672
So we know a lot of you may
have sensitive data in our cloud

00:44:42.672 --> 00:44:43.630
that you trust us with.

00:44:43.630 --> 00:44:44.860
We know many of
you may also have

00:44:44.860 --> 00:44:46.610
auditors who ask the
same question, right,

00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:48.380
as part of doing their job.

00:44:48.380 --> 00:44:50.500
And so here, the first
thing I want to start with

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:54.790
is, at Google Cloud, right,
we do not access your data

00:44:54.790 --> 00:44:57.160
for any other reason
other than to fulfill

00:44:57.160 --> 00:45:00.055
our contractual
obligations to you, ok?

00:45:00.055 --> 00:45:02.180
So that means whenever
there's a data access event,

00:45:02.180 --> 00:45:04.850
right, whenever someone
goes to access your data,

00:45:04.850 --> 00:45:09.577
it has to have a very strong
business justification.

00:45:09.577 --> 00:45:11.910
And it has to be in line with
that particular statement.

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:14.630
They are fulfilling our
contractual obligations to you.

00:45:14.630 --> 00:45:15.630
You can go online again.

00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:17.950
You can google
access transparency,

00:45:17.950 --> 00:45:20.880
and you can find out the
well-defined set of sub reasons

00:45:20.880 --> 00:45:22.360
for why we access your data.

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:24.910
And it turns out by far
the most common one,

00:45:24.910 --> 00:45:27.450
I think over 95% of
these access events

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:30.000
happen because our
customers, you guys,

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.540
asked us to access your data as
part of, say, a support case,

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:34.434
right?

00:45:34.434 --> 00:45:35.850
But on top of that,
you don't have

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:37.950
to take our word for it, right?

00:45:37.950 --> 00:45:39.630
Whenever Google
accesses your data,

00:45:39.630 --> 00:45:42.300
we'll create an audit log
for you in near real time,

00:45:42.300 --> 00:45:44.910
right, so that you can hold us
accountable to that statement

00:45:44.910 --> 00:45:47.400
there, right?

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:49.920
So let's take a quick
look at one of those audit

00:45:49.920 --> 00:45:51.210
logs looks like.

00:45:51.210 --> 00:45:53.070
The font's probably
a little small,

00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:55.445
so I'll just walk through this
audit log for those of you

00:45:55.445 --> 00:45:56.830
maybe in the back who can't see.

00:45:56.830 --> 00:45:57.750
So the first thing
you'll see is this

00:45:57.750 --> 00:45:59.140
is just a standard audit log.

00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:02.610
But it's created when an
access event happens, when

00:46:02.610 --> 00:46:04.200
a Google access event happens.

00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:06.366
And the first thing you'll
see highlighted in yellow

00:46:06.366 --> 00:46:10.980
there is we give you a reason
why we access your data.

00:46:10.980 --> 00:46:13.200
It aligns to one
of the sub reasons

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:15.450
for fulfilling our contractual
obligations to you.

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:16.950
And so here you see
this is actually

00:46:16.950 --> 00:46:20.040
a customer-initiated
support request.

00:46:20.040 --> 00:46:21.885
And we even give
you the case number

00:46:21.885 --> 00:46:24.600
so that you can follow up,
understand why exactly we're

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:25.710
accessing your data.

00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:27.461
Here, likely it's
because you asked us to.

00:46:27.461 --> 00:46:29.043
And then in green,
you see we give you

00:46:29.043 --> 00:46:30.810
a lot of information
around, what exactly

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:34.810
did Google do with your data
during this access event,

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:35.310
right?

00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:37.500
So which method did we call?

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:39.030
Which resource
did we call it on?

00:46:39.030 --> 00:46:40.050
How long did it take?

00:46:40.050 --> 00:46:42.285
What project did it happen
in, et cetera, right?

00:46:42.285 --> 00:46:44.160
And so this is really
aligning with our goal,

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:47.010
with our principle of making
it really transparent in how

00:46:47.010 --> 00:46:50.640
we administer our platform
and giving you, our customers,

00:46:50.640 --> 00:46:54.010
the tools to hold us
accountable for that, right?

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:57.196
Ok, so that brings
us to the end.

00:46:57.196 --> 00:46:58.570
Just to quickly
